  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nEarly morning radar trends shows the last of decaying convection\nfrom this past evening petering out over north-central West\nVirginia. Otherwise, only other item of note for early this\nmorning is some river valley fog development noted on nighttime\nsatellite imagery across our southern coalfields.\n\nWarming temperatures are on tap for today as predominantly south\nto southwesterly flow takes hold. Afternoon temperatures are\nprogged to reach the mid 60s to mid 70s along the mountains and\nlow to mid 80s across the lowlands. While remaining mostly dry\nacross our southern zones, a boundary quietly draped through the\nheart of the forecast area this morning will fire off diurnally\ndriven showers and thunderstorms near the north-central lowlands\nand northeast West Virginia mountains later this afternoon.\nLimited instability today will temper the severity of storms.\n\nFriday will feature slightly warmer temperatures as the region\nremains planted in the warm sector of an approaching low\npressure system. Ahead of this disturbance, mainly dry weather\nis anticipated once again, with the exception of an isolated\nchance for pop up shower and storm activity within aggravated\ncumulus fields along the mountains Friday afternoon.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nA cold front sliding through the Ohio Valley on Friday will\narrive to our far western zones late in the evening, with\nshowers and storms aligned along the boundary. Simulated radar\nfor this timeframe depicts convective activity reaching Perry\nCounty after sunset Friday evening, where a Marginal risk for\nsevere weather grazes the northwestern tip of the county. The\nloss of daytime heating at the time of this line's arrival will\nbegin weakening the strength of these storms as they slide\nthrough the forecast area overnight Friday into Saturday\nmorning. \n\nA secondary line of precipitation, denoting the location of the\nfront itself, slides into the Central Appalachians by Saturday \nmorning. Shower and storm chances quickly dash from west to east\nSaturday afternoon, leaving behind a few tenths of an inch of \nmuch needed rainfall to a drought stricken region so far this\nspring.\n\nA period of drier conditions settles in for the end of the\nweekend before active weather looms once again for the start of\nnext week. A few passing disturbances bring chances of showers\nand storms through the end of the 7 day forecast cycle.\nTemperatures appear to hover around their seasonable norms for\nthis time of year for the upcoming work week.",
